########################################
# DRAFT - NOT INSTALLED, NOT BUILT.
#
# The packet-class allows the captures call for. wip/ is not inside any
# package's files/ directory, so nothing here is compiled or loaded. To apply,
# move rules into selinux-desktop-home/files/desktop.home.te (or
# desktop.system.base.te, or a new module) and rebuild.
#
# Evidence - 59 `packet` denials across captures/:
#
#     39  staff_t          -> unlabeled_t          { send }
#     16  staff_t          -> unlabeled_t          { recv }
#      2  stremio_server_t -> ssh_client_packet_t  { send }
#      2  stremio_server_t -> ssh_client_packet_t  { recv }
#
# Neither allow exists in the policy today. The base module declares 471
# *_packet_t types, so labelled networking is available and working.
########################################

# --- staff_t -> unlabeled_t ------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything the secmark maps do not cover arrives unlabelled: the maps only
# label ports 22, 53, 80 and 443, so mosh (UDP 60001/60002, to
# 62.210.213.30) and all other traffic is checked as unlabeled_t. Two ways
# out - pick one, they are not equivalent:
#
# (A) allow unlabelled packets for the domain. This is the draft already
#     written and commented out in desktop.home.te:
#         #corenet_sendrecv_unlabeled_packets(staff_t) # MainThread?
#     Simple and honest about the maps being non-exhaustive - but it means the
#     secmark labels only ever restrict traffic on those four mapped ports.
corenet_sendrecv_unlabeled_packets(staff_t)

# (B) label mosh's ports instead, so the traffic is actually covered by a
#     secmark. Needs a secmark and map entries in files/ruleset.nftables:
#         secmark mosh_server { "system_u:object_r:<PACKET_TYPE>:s0" }
#         60001 : "mosh_server", 60002 : "mosh_server",  (secmapping_out)
#     There is no mosh_packet_t in the policy (checked: 0 hits), so
#     <PACKET_TYPE> is either a new type declared by a module, or an existing
#     generic packet type. That is a real addition, not a one-liner - and if
#     the answer is "declare a new type", the maps should probably be
#     restructured around policy types rather than around a fixed port list.

# --- stremio_server_t -> ssh_client_packet_t -------------------------------
#
# Stremio connects outward to :22 and secmapping_out labels port 22 as
# ssh_client, so its traffic is checked against ssh_client_packet_t.
# Currently masked by the permissive domain stremio_server_t.
allow stremio_server_t ssh_client_packet_t:packet { send recv };

# --- not covered by these captures ----------------------------------------
#
# The ruleset labels :53 as dns_client for both directions, and :80/:443 as
# http_server inbound / http_client outbound. Those labels exist but no
# capture shows a denial against them, either because the traffic is already
# allowed or because the domains that generate it are permissive. Which
# domains are permissive today:
#
#     crow_t gajim_t java_t mplayer_t staff_git_t staff_t stremio_server_t
#
# Note staff_t - the main desktop user domain - is one of them, so most of what
# these captures show is logged, not enforced. Re-check the packet evidence
# after staff_t leaves the permissive list; expect more of it.
